WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,028
The threat intelligence lifecycle is a practical way to keep CTI work connected to decisions.

2
00:00:06,028 --> 00:00:12,286
The stages are requirements, collection, processing, analysis, dissemination, and feedback.

3
00:00:12,286 --> 00:00:25,029
In real teams the flow is not always perfectly linear, but the model is useful because it reminds analysts that intelligence starts with a need and ends with whether the consumer could use the product.

4
00:00:25,029 --> 00:00:26,708
Requirements come first.

5
00:00:26,708 --> 00:00:34,720
An intelligence requirement is a clear statement of what the consumer needs to know in order to make a decision or take action.

6
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:37,620
Vague questions create noisy intelligence.

7
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:41,893
"Tell me about ransomware" is too broad for most operational work.

8
00:00:41,893 --> 00:00:52,500
Better requirements might ask which ransomware behaviors are relevant to the company's technology stack, which exposed services are being targeted in the sector,

9
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,934
or which detections should be prioritized this week.

10
00:00:55,934 --> 00:00:59,825
Requirements also keep the program honest about tradeoffs.

11
00:00:59,825 --> 00:01:02,267
CTI teams rarely have unlimited time.

12
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:09,822
If analysts collect broadly without requirements, they spend effort sorting data that may never support a decision.

13
00:01:09,822 --> 00:01:19,742
If they define requirements clearly, they can explain why they chose certain sources, why they ignored others, and what level of confidence is realistic.

14
00:01:19,742 --> 00:01:26,686
Requirements turn CTI from general monitoring into a service that answers defensible security questions.

15
00:01:26,686 --> 00:01:29,127
Collection follows the requirement.

16
00:01:29,127 --> 00:01:41,871
The analyst chooses sources that can plausibly answer the question: internal telemetry, incident reports, vendor reporting, vulnerability advisories, government notices, community sharing,

17
00:01:41,871 --> 00:01:46,220
malware reports, open-source research, or threat research blogs.

18
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:50,341
Collection discipline matters because every source adds cost.

19
00:01:50,341 --> 00:02:00,719
More sources can mean more duplication, more false positives, more licensing concerns, and more review work if the team has not defined what it is trying to learn.

20
00:02:00,719 --> 00:02:04,382
Processing turns collected material into a usable form.

21
00:02:04,382 --> 00:02:19,338
That may mean deduplicating indicators, normalizing timestamps, extracting affected products, mapping behavior to ATT&CK techniques, translating a long report into key claims, or separating confirmed facts from speculation.

22
00:02:19,338 --> 00:02:23,001
Analysis then turns processed material into a judgment.

23
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:31,852
The analyst evaluates source reliability, corroboration, recency, relevance to the local environment, assumptions, and confidence.

24
00:02:31,852 --> 00:02:42,612
Dissemination delivers the product in the format the audience can act on: a brief, ticket, detection story, watchlist, executive summary, or incident response note.

25
00:02:42,612 --> 00:02:44,291
Feedback closes the loop.

26
00:02:44,291 --> 00:02:46,580
Did the SOC use the triage guidance?

27
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:48,716
Did the detection find anything?

28
00:02:48,716 --> 00:02:51,082
Did leadership understand the risk?

29
00:02:51,082 --> 00:02:54,439
Did the vulnerability team need different context?

30
00:02:54,439 --> 00:03:00,926
Feedback prevents CTI from becoming a publishing function and keeps it tied to real defensive needs.

31
00:03:00,926 --> 00:03:03,978
The lifecycle also gives teams a way to improve.

32
00:03:03,978 --> 00:03:12,143
If a product was ignored, the problem may have been the requirement, the source choice, the analysis, the format, or the timing.

33
00:03:12,143 --> 00:03:17,332
Reviewing each stage turns misses into process improvements rather than blame.

34
00:03:17,332 --> 00:03:22,597
That is how a CTI function matures from ad hoc reporting into a repeatable service.
